    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d481">

                <pb facs="0541" n="533" sameAs="frus:frus1939v02/pg533"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851.711/367: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Marseille</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hurley</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Marseille</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-09-15T18:00:00+01:00">September 15, 1939—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 15—2:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>21. Naval authorities announced their intention of censoring all mail and
                    removing any addressed to Germany on board SS <hi rendition="#italic">President
                        Adams</hi> all of which originated in India bound for New York. Embassy was
                    informed. The Consulate explained to the officer in <pb facs="0542" n="534"
                        xml:id="pg534"/> charge the Department’s attitude as set forth in
                        Hyde,<anchor n="67" xml:id="d481-f1"/> Section 730, <ref target="#pg446"
                        >page 446</ref>. Officer thereupon contented himself with verifying origin
                    and destination of bags without opening. The Consulate’s despatch
                        follows.<anchor n="68" xml:id="d481-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hurley</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
